seg-5de2e6656527 = The capstone. You will run a spoofing-detector scenario and a full fused positioning, navigation and timing (PNT) suite, and learn to read <b>security</b>, <b>integrity</b>, and <b>dead-reckoning</b> together — the whole thesis: quantum inertial + optical timing buy resilience that GNSS (Global Navigation Satellite System) denial would otherwise take away.

# text
seg-ffa5242638c2 = Part A — Security: catching a time spoof

# text
# c1: 1 − P_md
seg-23c18da1415f = An attacker injects a slowly-ramping false GNSS time ({ "0.1" } ns/s, starting at t = { "60" } s). The receiver cross-checks the asserted time against its own clock’s coasted prediction and flags the spoof when the discrepancy passes the detection bound. The detector is a two-sided <b>χ²₁ / Neyman–Pearson energy test</b> at a false-alarm probability P_fa = { "0.01" }; the Security figure of merit is <b><c1/></b> (one minus the probability of missed detection) at the spec-sized ({ "20" } ns) spoof magnitude.

# text
seg-7806a929dbda = Summary:

# text
# c1: security 1.000
seg-46aa0e01b330 = <b>Quantum: <c1/>, detected at { "70" } s.</b> The optical clock’s detection floor is a fraction of a nanosecond, so it flags the spoof almost immediately — far below the { "20" } ns spec.

# text
# c1: security 0.558
seg-022573b3dcc4 = <b>Classical: <c1/>, detected at { "430" } s.</b> The chip-scale atomic clock’s (CSAC’s) own coast noise over the window is comparable to { "20" } ns, so it often can’t tell the spoof from its own drift — it misses { "44" } % of the time.

